{%- set sums = release.checksums | default('SHA256SUMS') -%}
{%- set download = 'file' if release.files else 'archive' -%}
# Verify a release

{% if visibility == 'public' -%}
The [release workflow](../.github/workflows/release.yml) builds every release on GitHub Actions
from its tag and attests where it came from. Check that before you run it.
{%- elif kind == 'binary' -%}
The [release workflow](../.github/workflows/release.yml) builds every release on GitHub Actions
from its tag and lists each {{ 'file' if release.files else 'archive' }}'s checksum. Check a download against it before you run it. The
checksums sit in the same release as the {{ 'files' if release.files else 'archives' }}, so they catch a broken download, not a swapped
one. The releases carry no attestation.
{%- else -%}
The [release workflow](../.github/workflows/release.yml) builds every release on GitHub Actions
from its tag and names the image it pushed by its digest. The image is neither attested nor signed.
{%- endif %}
{% if kind == 'binary' %}
## Check the checksum

In the directory with the download and the release's `{{ sums }}`:

```sh
sha256sum --check --ignore-missing {{ sums }}
```

On macOS, use `shasum -a 256 --check --ignore-missing {{ sums }}`.
{%- if 'windows' in platforms %} On Windows, run
`(Get-FileHash <{{ download }}>).Hash` in PowerShell and compare it with the {{ download }}'s line in
`{{ sums }}`; the case doesn't matter.
{%- endif %}
{%- if visibility == 'public' %}

## Check the attestation

Download the release's `attestation.json` too. Then, with the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/):

```sh
gh attestation verify <{{ 'file' if release.files else 'archive' }}> --bundle attestation.json \
  --repo {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }} \
  --signer-workflow {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}/.github/workflows/release.yml \
  --source-ref refs/heads/main
```

It needs no GitHub sign-in. It fails unless this repository's `release.yml`, running on `main`,
attested that exact file.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
## Find the digest

Each release's notes on GitHub name the image by its digest, such as
`ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@sha256:<digest>`.
{%- if visibility == 'public' %} Verify that digest and run it.
A tag such as `latest` or `1.4` can move to another image after you check it; a digest can't.
{%- else %} Run that digest to get that release
exactly. A tag such as `latest` or `1.4` can move to another image; a digest can't.
{%- endif %}
{%- if visibility == 'public' %}

## Check the signature

With [cosign](https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/system_config/installation/):

```sh
cosign verify ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@sha256:<digest> \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  --certificate-identity https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/heads/main
```

It fails unless this repository's `release.yml`, running on `main`, signed that image.

## Check the attestation

With the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/), signed in with `gh auth login`:

```sh
gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@sha256:<digest> \
  --repo {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }} \
  --signer-workflow {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}/.github/workflows/release.yml \
  --source-ref refs/heads/main
```

The release workflow runs both checks on the digest before it tags the image with its version,
minor and `latest`, so every tag names an image that passed them.
{%- endif %}

## Run the digest

```sh
docker run --rm ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@sha256:<digest>
```
{%- endif %}
